WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:01.820
Hi, I'm Nei Maldaner,

2
00:00:01.820 --> 00:00:03.730
and this is module four of

3
00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:04.500
OSWork.

4
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:05.300
In module three,

5
00:00:05.300 --> 00:00:07.200
you opened the terminal and took your

6
00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:09.000
first steps with Codex in a practice

7
00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:10.040
folder.

8
00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:11.180
Now we're going to put the house

9
00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:12.840
in order around that work.

10
00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:13.720
In this module,

11
00:00:13.720 --> 00:00:15.800
you'll create a folder tree that keeps

12
00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.240
one topic apart from another,

13
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.480
write a README in Markdown that explains

14
00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.220
the project to whoever comes later,

15
00:00:24.280 --> 00:00:26.060
give each kind of note its own

16
00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.860
file, and keep passwords and keys far

17
00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:31.340
away from everything that gets shared.

18
00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:32.970
At the end,

19
00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:34.840
you'll write down what the AI should

20
00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:36.320
read first.

21
00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:39.040
Denise, a pedagogical coordinator,

22
00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:41.420
and Lúcia, an eighth grade science teacher,

23
00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:42.600
are still with us.

24
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:45.100
Every practice uses the same terminal from

25
00:00:45.100 --> 00:00:46.820
module three, with

26
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:48.140
made-up data.

27
00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:49.120
When I ask,

28
00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.500
pause the video and do it right

29
00:00:50.500 --> 00:00:51.000
then.

30
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:52.940
Think of the steel filing cabinet in

31
00:00:52.940 --> 00:00:53.640
the school office.

32
00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:55.860
Each drawer holds one class,

33
00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:57.280
and nobody looks for 8th grade A

34
00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:59.060
in the 7th grade B drawer.

35
00:00:59.060 --> 00:01:01.368
A work folder plays the same role:

36
00:01:01.368 --> 00:01:02.928
it gathers the material for a single

37
00:01:02.928 --> 00:01:03.588
topic.

38
00:01:03.588 --> 00:01:05.368
That material is the context of the

39
00:01:05.368 --> 00:01:06.008
task.

40
00:01:06.008 --> 00:01:07.668
When you open Codex in a folder,

41
00:01:07.668 --> 00:01:09.388
it works from that folder.

42
00:01:09.388 --> 00:01:11.328
If the folder mixes topics,

43
00:01:11.328 --> 00:01:14.208
anything unrelated to the request becomes noise.

44
00:01:14.208 --> 00:01:16.308
Lúcia kept the science fair project in

45
00:01:16.308 --> 00:01:18.528
the same folder as the report cards.

46
00:01:18.528 --> 00:01:20.208
She asked the AI for a summary

47
00:01:20.208 --> 00:01:22.108
of the fair and got a paragraph

48
00:01:22.108 --> 00:01:24.868
with a student's grade in the middle.

49
00:01:24.868 --> 00:01:26.208
Once the fair folder held only the

50
00:01:26.208 --> 00:01:27.808
rules and the list of groups,

51
00:01:27.808 --> 00:01:29.888
the summary talked only about the fair.

52
00:01:29.888 --> 00:01:32.388
The AI reads less material,

53
00:01:32.388 --> 00:01:34.288
and you know where every sentence came

54
00:01:34.288 --> 00:01:35.368
from.

55
00:01:35.368 --> 00:01:36.388
In the terminal,

56
00:01:36.388 --> 00:01:38.288
the tilde symbol stands for your home

57
00:01:38.288 --> 00:01:39.288
folder.

58
00:01:39.288 --> 00:01:40.688
It's just a short way of writing

59
00:01:40.688 --> 00:01:41.868
the full address,

60
00:01:41.868 --> 00:01:44.368
which changes from one computer to another.

61
00:01:44.368 --> 00:01:46.868
Denise typed the pwd command,

62
00:01:46.868 --> 00:01:47.668
like in module three,

63
00:01:47.668 --> 00:01:50.008
and saw that whole address.

64
00:01:50.328 --> 00:01:51.718
Inside your home folder,

65
00:01:51.718 --> 00:01:54.008
you'll create a folder called projetos.

66
00:01:54.008 --> 00:01:54.848
Two things go in it.

67
00:01:54.848 --> 00:01:56.148
A config folder,

68
00:01:56.148 --> 00:01:58.208
short for configuration,

69
00:01:58.208 --> 00:02:00.008
with whatever applies to any project,

70
00:02:00.008 --> 00:02:00.748
like your preferences.

71
00:02:00.748 --> 00:02:01.808
And

72
00:02:01.808 --> 00:02:04.636
each project, with its own entradas folder,

73
00:02:04.636 --> 00:02:06.256
the inputs the AI reads,

74
00:02:06.256 --> 00:02:07.896
and its own saidas folder,

75
00:02:07.896 --> 00:02:09.536
the outputs it produces.

76
00:02:09.536 --> 00:02:11.316
Kept apart, you can check one against

77
00:02:11.316 --> 00:02:12.656
the other.

78
00:02:12.816 --> 00:02:15.556
Denise prefers short reports with the open

79
00:02:15.556 --> 00:02:16.896
items at the end,

80
00:02:16.896 --> 00:02:18.656
both for the council report and

81
00:02:18.656 --> 00:02:20.176
for the parents meeting agenda.

82
00:02:20.176 --> 00:02:22.596
That's why this preference goes into config,

83
00:02:22.596 --> 00:02:23.096
just once.

84
00:02:22.916 --> 00:02:23.876
Before

85
00:02:23.876 --> 00:02:25.496
you create anything,

86
00:02:25.856 --> 00:02:27.336
sketch the tree on paper:

87
00:02:27.336 --> 00:02:29.536
a config folder and a single practice

88
00:02:29.536 --> 00:02:30.456
project.

89
00:02:30.456 --> 00:02:32.736
That way you choose the names calmly.

90
00:02:32.736 --> 00:02:33.236
Use short names,

91
00:02:33.236 --> 00:02:36.216
with no spaces and no accents,

92
00:02:36.216 --> 00:02:38.696
because in the terminal spaces and accents

93
00:02:38.696 --> 00:02:41.416
cause trouble in every command.

94
00:02:41.556 --> 00:02:43.876
Lúcia sketched hers on a napkin and,

95
00:02:43.876 --> 00:02:44.716
in one minute,

96
00:02:44.716 --> 00:02:47.456
avoided three folders with similar names.

97
00:02:47.456 --> 00:02:50.056
Then comes the mkdir command with the

98
00:02:50.056 --> 00:02:51.516
dash p option,

99
00:02:51.516 --> 00:02:53.656
which creates the folders plus any parent

100
00:02:53.656 --> 00:02:55.096
folders that are missing.

101
00:02:55.096 --> 00:02:56.876
If a folder already exists,

102
00:02:56.876 --> 00:02:58.176
it stays as it is,

103
00:02:58.176 --> 00:03:00.156
and nothing gets deleted.

104
00:03:00.156 --> 00:03:01.976
Pause the video and do it now.

105
00:03:01.976 --> 00:03:03.176
Sketch the tree,

106
00:03:03.176 --> 00:03:04.704
open the terminal from module three,

107
00:03:04.704 --> 00:03:06.424
and paste the three lines on the

108
00:03:06.424 --> 00:03:07.384
screen.

109
00:03:07.384 --> 00:03:09.824
Check that the first ls shows config

110
00:03:09.824 --> 00:03:11.164
and meu primeiro projeto,

111
00:03:11.164 --> 00:03:13.584
and that the second one shows entradas

112
00:03:13.584 --> 00:03:14.624
and saidas.

113
00:03:14.624 --> 00:03:15.704
The folder exists,

114
00:03:15.704 --> 00:03:17.964
but whoever comes later doesn't know what

115
00:03:17.964 --> 00:03:19.224
it's for.

116
00:03:19.224 --> 00:03:20.864
To write that down,

117
00:03:20.864 --> 00:03:22.804
we'll use Markdown.

118
00:03:22.904 --> 00:03:25.204
Every one of Lúcia's experiment guides has

119
00:03:25.204 --> 00:03:26.764
a title, materials,

120
00:03:26.764 --> 00:03:28.224
and procedure.

121
00:03:28.224 --> 00:03:30.424
She underlines the titles and puts a

122
00:03:30.424 --> 00:03:32.444
dash before each material.

123
00:03:33.244 --> 00:03:35.144
Markdown does the same thing with symbols

124
00:03:35.144 --> 00:03:35.904
you type.

125
00:03:35.904 --> 00:03:37.764
A hash sign at the start of

126
00:03:37.764 --> 00:03:39.724
a line makes a title,

127
00:03:39.724 --> 00:03:42.224
two hash signs make a subtitle,

128
00:03:42.224 --> 00:03:44.044
and a hyphen makes a list item.

129
00:03:44.044 --> 00:03:46.184
The file is still plain text,

130
00:03:46.184 --> 00:03:47.904
so you can read it even without

131
00:03:47.904 --> 00:03:49.544
a special program.

132
00:03:50.244 --> 00:03:52.904
Lúcia moved her bean germination guide to

133
00:03:52.904 --> 00:03:54.764
Markdown in five minutes,

134
00:03:54.764 --> 00:03:57.524
without learning anything beyond those three marks.

135
00:03:57.524 --> 00:03:59.564
A Markdown file is a text file

136
00:03:59.564 --> 00:04:01.284
whose name ends in dot md,

137
00:04:01.284 --> 00:04:03.444
and it opens in any editor,

138
00:04:03.444 --> 00:04:04.664
including the terminal.

139
00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:06.960
The file that explains the project is

140
00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:08.440
called README.

141
00:04:08.500 --> 00:04:11.680
The course kit template has five sections:

142
00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:13.600
purpose, read first,

143
00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:14.800
organization,

144
00:04:14.800 --> 00:04:15.680
how to check,

145
00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:17.460
and current status.

146
00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.040
You replace each template text with what's

147
00:04:20.040 --> 00:04:21.500
true for your project.

148
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:22.560
And if you don't know a field

149
00:04:22.560 --> 00:04:23.760
yet?

150
00:04:24.340 --> 00:04:25.780
Write to be defined.

151
00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:27.760
An honest open field is better than

152
00:04:27.760 --> 00:04:30.140
template text nobody checked.

153
00:04:30.140 --> 00:04:31.700
In the science fair README,

154
00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:33.780
Lúcia wrote under purpose:

155
00:04:33.780 --> 00:04:36.060
organize sign-ups for the fair.

156
00:04:36.060 --> 00:04:37.940
And under how to check:

157
00:04:38.120 --> 00:04:40.380
every registered group appears on the final

158
00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:41.460
list.

159
00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:42.220
You don't need bold,

160
00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:44.320
tables, or links.

161
00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:46.080
The value comes from clarity.

162
00:04:46.080 --> 00:04:47.720
When Denise took a week off,

163
00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:49.360
the colleague covering for her opened the

164
00:04:49.360 --> 00:04:51.340
council project README and kept the work

165
00:04:51.340 --> 00:04:53.600
going without having to call her.

166
00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:54.900
To edit in the terminal,

167
00:04:54.900 --> 00:04:55.840
use nano.

168
00:04:55.840 --> 00:04:57.020
It shows the text in the middle

169
00:04:57.020 --> 00:04:58.900
and the shortcuts at the bottom.

170
00:04:58.900 --> 00:05:01.180
The caret symbol means Control.

171
00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:03.640
You move around with the arrow keys,

172
00:05:03.640 --> 00:05:05.520
save with Control O,

173
00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:06.800
confirm the name with Enter,

174
00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:09.161
and exit with Control X.

175
00:05:09.161 --> 00:05:11.141
Then the cat command shows on screen

176
00:05:11.141 --> 00:05:12.241
what was saved.

177
00:05:12.241 --> 00:05:14.121
Pause the video and practice.

178
00:05:14.121 --> 00:05:16.881
Go into the practice project folder,

179
00:05:17.481 --> 00:05:20.021
download the README template with the curl

180
00:05:20.021 --> 00:05:22.441
command, and open it in nano.

181
00:05:22.441 --> 00:05:24.201
Heads up:

182
00:05:24.201 --> 00:05:26.541
if you've already written a README in

183
00:05:26.541 --> 00:05:29.161
that folder, skip the curl line,

184
00:05:29.161 --> 00:05:31.191
because it writes over the file.

185
00:05:31.191 --> 00:05:33.261
Replace each template text with something true

186
00:05:33.261 --> 00:05:34.341
about your project,

187
00:05:34.341 --> 00:05:36.201
or with to be defined,

188
00:05:36.201 --> 00:05:39.281
and describe the entradas and saidas folders.

189
00:05:39.461 --> 00:05:42.061
Save, exit, and use cat to check

190
00:05:42.061 --> 00:05:43.441
that the titles still have their hash

191
00:05:43.441 --> 00:05:44.461
signs.

192
00:05:44.461 --> 00:05:46.641
The README talks about one project.

193
00:05:46.641 --> 00:05:49.101
What about things that apply to everything,

194
00:05:49.101 --> 00:05:51.341
like your preferences and decisions?

195
00:05:51.341 --> 00:05:54.641
The school office doesn't write everything in

196
00:05:54.641 --> 00:05:56.261
a single notebook:

197
00:05:56.741 --> 00:05:58.821
it has the minutes book,

198
00:05:58.821 --> 00:06:00.321
the incidents book,

199
00:06:00.321 --> 00:06:01.761
and the procedures notebook,

200
00:06:01.761 --> 00:06:04.421
and each one answers one question.

201
00:06:04.421 --> 00:06:06.321
In the config folder,

202
00:06:06.321 --> 00:06:08.761
four files play that role.

203
00:06:08.771 --> 00:06:10.301
memoria dot md answers how I like

204
00:06:10.301 --> 00:06:11.723
the work to come out.

205
00:06:11.723 --> 00:06:13.403
decisoes dot md,

206
00:06:13.403 --> 00:06:15.043
what we chose,

207
00:06:15.043 --> 00:06:16.413
when, and why.

208
00:06:16.413 --> 00:06:17.883
dicas dot md,

209
00:06:17.883 --> 00:06:19.523
how it's done,

210
00:06:19.523 --> 00:06:21.403
the way that has already worked.

211
00:06:21.403 --> 00:06:22.443
And falhas dot md,

212
00:06:22.443 --> 00:06:24.363
what went wrong and what the fix

213
00:06:24.363 --> 00:06:25.183
was.

214
00:06:25.183 --> 00:06:26.603
Denise was trying to find out why

215
00:06:26.603 --> 00:06:28.863
the parents meeting had moved to Saturday.

216
00:06:28.863 --> 00:06:31.023
The reason was in a forty-page notebook,

217
00:06:31.023 --> 00:06:33.003
among messages and phone numbers,

218
00:06:33.003 --> 00:06:34.963
and it took her half an hour

219
00:06:34.963 --> 00:06:36.003
to find it.

220
00:06:36.003 --> 00:06:37.943
A preference is your steady way of

221
00:06:37.943 --> 00:06:40.863
working, and it hardly ever changes.

222
00:06:40.863 --> 00:06:43.223
Lúcia prefers exercises with the answer key

223
00:06:43.223 --> 00:06:44.483
at the end:

224
00:06:44.483 --> 00:06:46.183
that goes in memoria.

225
00:06:46.183 --> 00:06:47.783
A decision is a choice with a

226
00:06:47.783 --> 00:06:50.283
reason, and the reason can change.

227
00:06:50.283 --> 00:06:52.103
The 8th grade tests have ten questions

228
00:06:52.103 --> 00:06:54.783
because the coordination standardized them:

229
00:06:54.783 --> 00:06:56.463
that goes in decisoes,

230
00:06:56.463 --> 00:06:57.423
with a date.

231
00:06:57.423 --> 00:06:58.763
When a decision changes,

232
00:06:58.763 --> 00:07:00.703
don't quietly replace the old line.

233
00:07:00.703 --> 00:07:03.343
In October, the tests went to twelve

234
00:07:03.343 --> 00:07:04.383
questions,

235
00:07:04.383 --> 00:07:06.743
with two on reading graphs.

236
00:07:06.943 --> 00:07:08.883
Lúcia added the new line and marked

237
00:07:08.883 --> 00:07:11.083
the August one as replaced.

238
00:07:11.143 --> 00:07:13.663
The falhas file records the symptom,

239
00:07:13.663 --> 00:07:14.923
the confirmed cause,

240
00:07:14.923 --> 00:07:16.024
and the smallest fix.

241
00:07:16.024 --> 00:07:18.444
The minutes summary came out empty because

242
00:07:18.444 --> 00:07:20.724
the entradas folder was empty.

243
00:07:20.764 --> 00:07:22.224
The fix was to check the folder

244
00:07:22.224 --> 00:07:23.564
before asking.

245
00:07:23.564 --> 00:07:25.584
You open this file when the problem

246
00:07:25.584 --> 00:07:26.744
comes back, without

247
00:07:26.744 --> 00:07:28.784
pasting it into every request.

248
00:07:28.784 --> 00:07:30.764
Sometimes a note seems to fit in

249
00:07:30.764 --> 00:07:32.004
two files.

250
00:07:32.804 --> 00:07:33.804
Then ask:

251
00:07:33.804 --> 00:07:35.984
does it say how I like it,

252
00:07:35.984 --> 00:07:37.104
what we chose,

253
00:07:37.104 --> 00:07:38.144
how it's done,

254
00:07:38.144 --> 00:07:39.764
or what went wrong?

255
00:07:39.764 --> 00:07:42.224
The first answer that fits decides.

256
00:07:42.464 --> 00:07:44.364
Pause the video and practice.

257
00:07:44.364 --> 00:07:46.204
Go into the config folder and download

258
00:07:46.204 --> 00:07:48.084
the four kit templates with the curl

259
00:07:48.084 --> 00:07:49.284
command,

260
00:07:49.304 --> 00:07:51.324
one line for each file.

261
00:07:51.324 --> 00:07:52.664
If you've already written in any of

262
00:07:52.664 --> 00:07:54.444
them, skip its line,

263
00:07:54.444 --> 00:07:56.731
because curl writes over the file.

264
00:07:56.731 --> 00:07:59.404
The ls should show all four files.

265
00:07:59.404 --> 00:08:00.844
Then, on paper,

266
00:08:00.844 --> 00:08:02.584
say where each of the five made-up

267
00:08:02.584 --> 00:08:03.824
notes from the lesson belongs,

268
00:08:03.824 --> 00:08:06.364
like I prefer notices to families in

269
00:08:06.364 --> 00:08:07.964
five lines or fewer,

270
00:08:07.964 --> 00:08:09.584
or report cards in PDF since September,

271
00:08:09.584 --> 00:08:10.644
because

272
00:08:10.644 --> 00:08:12.964
not every family opens spreadsheets.

273
00:08:12.984 --> 00:08:15.044
Write down the reason for each choice

274
00:08:15.044 --> 00:08:16.947
and compare with the answer key.

275
00:08:17.240 --> 00:08:18.980
And the school system password,

276
00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:20.240
which of those four files does it

277
00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:21.260
go in?

278
00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:22.760
None of them.

279
00:08:23.200 --> 00:08:24.500
At the front desk,

280
00:08:24.500 --> 00:08:26.700
the key board shows which rooms exist,

281
00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:28.600
and nobody worries about that list being

282
00:08:28.600 --> 00:08:29.460
visible.

283
00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:31.500
The keys are a different story:

284
00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:34.000
whoever takes the key opens the room.

285
00:08:34.000 --> 00:08:35.920
A credential works like the key.

286
00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:37.440
A password, an API key,

287
00:08:37.440 --> 00:08:39.980
or a bot token lets whoever holds

288
00:08:39.980 --> 00:08:42.300
it act on behalf of your account.

289
00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:43.660
Denise was about to send the team

290
00:08:43.660 --> 00:08:45.720
group a screenshot of the grading system

291
00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:46.920
settings.

292
00:08:47.140 --> 00:08:48.260
Before sending it,

293
00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:49.600
she saw the password for the entire

294
00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:52.060
coordination in the corner of the image.

295
00:08:52.060 --> 00:08:53.580
If she had sent it,

296
00:08:53.580 --> 00:08:55.080
all thirty people in the group would

297
00:08:55.080 --> 00:08:56.380
have had access.

298
00:08:56.380 --> 00:08:57.320
She covered the password,

299
00:08:57.320 --> 00:08:59.280
and the team saw what they needed,

300
00:08:59.280 --> 00:09:01.840
with access still limited to the coordination.

301
00:09:01.940 --> 00:09:03.980
So where does the credential go?

302
00:09:03.980 --> 00:09:06.200
In a file called dot env.

303
00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:07.520
It holds variables:

304
00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:08.860
a name,

305
00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:09.520
an equals sign,

306
00:09:09.520 --> 00:09:11.080
and a value,

307
00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:13.020
like a Telegram bot token or a

308
00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:16.100
database address with the password inside.

309
00:09:16.240 --> 00:09:20.200
Heads up: dot env is not encrypted.

310
00:09:20.200 --> 00:09:22.000
Anyone with access to the file can

311
00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:23.360
read what's in it,

312
00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:25.660
so it stays only on your machine.

313
00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:27.020
In module seven,

314
00:09:27.020 --> 00:09:28.400
Lúcia will create a lookup bot for

315
00:09:28.400 --> 00:09:30.380
her class, and its token will live

316
00:09:30.380 --> 00:09:32.100
there, and nowhere else.

317
00:09:32.100 --> 00:09:33.610
To share the project,

318
00:09:33.610 --> 00:09:34.960
there's dot env dot example,

319
00:09:34.960 --> 00:09:38.100
with the same names and made-up values.

320
00:09:38.640 --> 00:09:40.580
Whoever receives it sees what they need

321
00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:41.120
to fill in,

322
00:09:41.120 --> 00:09:43.060
but gets access to nothing.

323
00:09:43.060 --> 00:09:44.840
That's how Denise handed the reports project

324
00:09:44.840 --> 00:09:46.460
over to the vice principal,

325
00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:48.020
and the vice principal filled in her

326
00:09:48.020 --> 00:09:48.740
own dot env.

327
00:09:48.740 --> 00:09:50.160
To help you,

328
00:09:50.160 --> 00:09:52.020
the AI almost always only needs to

329
00:09:52.020 --> 00:09:53.840
know that the variable exists.

330
00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:56.000
Lúcia's report wouldn't connect to the school

331
00:09:56.000 --> 00:09:57.200
spreadsheet.

332
00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:59.080
Instead of pasting the dot env,

333
00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:01.380
she wrote that the database variable was

334
00:10:01.380 --> 00:10:04.160
filled in and asked what to check.

335
00:10:04.160 --> 00:10:05.780
The help was just as good,

336
00:10:05.780 --> 00:10:07.620
and the secret stayed at home.

337
00:10:07.620 --> 00:10:09.240
If you paste a key by mistake,

338
00:10:09.240 --> 00:10:11.900
deleting the message isn't enough:

339
00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:13.400
replace the key on the site that

340
00:10:13.400 --> 00:10:14.740
generated it.

341
00:10:14.740 --> 00:10:16.600
Pause the video and practice.

342
00:10:16.600 --> 00:10:18.256
In the practice project folder,

343
00:10:18.256 --> 00:10:19.576
paste the lines on the screen all

344
00:10:19.576 --> 00:10:21.756
at once, without typing them by hand,

345
00:10:21.756 --> 00:10:23.536
because of the quotes.

346
00:10:23.536 --> 00:10:26.196
The printf command writes the two names,

347
00:10:26.196 --> 00:10:28.631
with the value preencha localmente,

348
00:10:28.631 --> 00:10:31.156
into dot env dot example.

349
00:10:31.156 --> 00:10:33.976
Check with cat and with ls dash

350
00:10:33.976 --> 00:10:35.176
a.

351
00:10:35.316 --> 00:10:37.276
Don't create the dot env now:

352
00:10:37.276 --> 00:10:39.956
you'll only need it in module seven.

353
00:10:39.956 --> 00:10:41.861
Now, how do you make sure dot

354
00:10:41.861 --> 00:10:44.016
env is never saved along with the

355
00:10:44.016 --> 00:10:46.796
project's history, not even by accident?

356
00:10:46.796 --> 00:10:48.876
Each version saved by Git is called

357
00:10:48.876 --> 00:10:50.046
a commit.

358
00:10:50.046 --> 00:10:52.676
Dot gitignore is a text file in

359
00:10:52.676 --> 00:10:54.456
the project folder with the list of

360
00:10:54.456 --> 00:10:55.296
what Git should leave out.

361
00:10:55.296 --> 00:10:58.376
That list includes dot env,

362
00:10:58.376 --> 00:11:00.456
its private variants,

363
00:11:00.476 --> 00:11:03.436
and the temporary folders that programs create

364
00:11:03.436 --> 00:11:04.416
on their own.

365
00:11:04.416 --> 00:11:06.596
Everything else goes into the history:

366
00:11:06.596 --> 00:11:08.556
the README, dot env dot example,

367
00:11:08.556 --> 00:11:10.696
and the rest of the work files.

368
00:11:10.696 --> 00:11:12.196
In the reports project,

369
00:11:12.196 --> 00:11:14.236
Denise put dot env on the list

370
00:11:14.236 --> 00:11:16.476
before saving the first version.

371
00:11:16.476 --> 00:11:17.676
The result:

372
00:11:17.676 --> 00:11:19.496
the file with the grading system password

373
00:11:19.496 --> 00:11:22.036
never entered the history.

374
00:11:22.036 --> 00:11:24.656
Git itself only comes in module six.

375
00:11:24.656 --> 00:11:26.881
For today, it's enough to have dot

376
00:11:26.881 --> 00:11:29.096
gitignore ready in the folder.

377
00:11:29.096 --> 00:11:30.916
There's a rule about order that trips

378
00:11:30.916 --> 00:11:32.543
up a lot of people.

379
00:11:32.543 --> 00:11:34.296
Dot gitignore doesn't apply to a file

380
00:11:34.296 --> 00:11:35.916
that has already been saved.

381
00:11:35.916 --> 00:11:37.896
It's like the school office courier pouch:

382
00:11:37.896 --> 00:11:40.096
the paper that shouldn't go out comes

383
00:11:40.096 --> 00:11:41.936
off the pile before the pouch is

384
00:11:41.936 --> 00:11:42.776
sealed.

385
00:11:42.776 --> 00:11:44.876
Once the pouch has left,

386
00:11:44.876 --> 00:11:46.436
crossing the paper off the list doesn't

387
00:11:46.436 --> 00:11:47.696
bring it back.

388
00:11:47.696 --> 00:11:49.776
A colleague of Denise's added dot env

389
00:11:49.776 --> 00:11:51.636
to gitignore a week after the first

390
00:11:51.636 --> 00:11:53.696
version, and the file with the password

391
00:11:53.696 --> 00:11:54.456
was still there,

392
00:11:54.456 --> 00:11:55.716
in the old version.

393
00:11:55.716 --> 00:11:56.516
And if a key leaked,

394
00:11:56.516 --> 00:11:58.836
the first fix is to revoke it

395
00:11:58.836 --> 00:11:59.496
at the source,

396
00:11:59.496 --> 00:12:01.176
meaning cancel it on the site that

397
00:12:01.176 --> 00:12:01.676
created it.

398
00:12:01.656 --> 00:12:02.856
The

399
00:12:03.416 --> 00:12:05.456
API key for one of Lúcia's projects

400
00:12:05.456 --> 00:12:07.476
showed up in a version shared with

401
00:12:07.476 --> 00:12:07.976
the team.

402
00:12:07.756 --> 00:12:11.156
She canceled it that same minute,

403
00:12:11.156 --> 00:12:12.316
created a new one,

404
00:12:12.316 --> 00:12:14.316
and only then dealt with the file.

405
00:12:14.316 --> 00:12:16.776
Fixing the history comes in module six.

406
00:12:17.100 --> 00:12:18.640
Each line in dot gitignore is a

407
00:12:18.640 --> 00:12:19.345
pattern.

408
00:12:19.345 --> 00:12:20.880
The dot env line catches the dot

409
00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:22.097
env file.

410
00:12:22.097 --> 00:12:24.100
The dot env dot asterisk line catches

411
00:12:24.100 --> 00:12:26.560
variants like dot env dot local,

412
00:12:26.560 --> 00:12:28.940
because the asterisk stands for any ending.

413
00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:30.200
And the line that starts with an

414
00:12:30.200 --> 00:12:32.760
exclamation mark makes an exception:

415
00:12:32.760 --> 00:12:35.800
it puts dot env dot example back

416
00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:37.660
on the list of what gets saved.

417
00:12:37.660 --> 00:12:39.580
Lúcia found that exclamation mark odd in

418
00:12:39.580 --> 00:12:40.640
the kit file,

419
00:12:40.640 --> 00:12:41.900
but it was what kept the example

420
00:12:41.900 --> 00:12:43.280
in the project,

421
00:12:43.580 --> 00:12:46.020
so her math colleague would know what

422
00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:47.160
to fill in.

423
00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:49.460
The last lines cover temporary folders,

424
00:12:49.460 --> 00:12:51.060
and you don't need to touch them.

425
00:12:51.060 --> 00:12:53.140
Pause the video and practice.

426
00:12:53.300 --> 00:12:55.480
Download the kit template with curl and

427
00:12:55.480 --> 00:12:58.100
display it with cat dash n.

428
00:12:58.100 --> 00:12:59.800
On paper, write down the number of

429
00:12:59.800 --> 00:13:01.740
the line that keeps dot env out

430
00:13:01.740 --> 00:13:03.500
and the one that keeps the example

431
00:13:03.500 --> 00:13:04.240
in.

432
00:13:04.240 --> 00:13:06.020
With everything in its place,

433
00:13:06.020 --> 00:13:08.040
what should the AI read first?

434
00:13:08.040 --> 00:13:10.480
It doesn't automatically read every Markdown file

435
00:13:10.480 --> 00:13:11.740
on your computer.

436
00:13:11.740 --> 00:13:14.200
You tell it which documents to consult.

437
00:13:14.240 --> 00:13:15.860
Denise asked for a draft of the

438
00:13:15.860 --> 00:13:18.660
council report without naming any file.

439
00:13:18.660 --> 00:13:20.351
She got back a table with each

440
00:13:20.351 --> 00:13:23.211
class's average and three recommendations,

441
00:13:23.211 --> 00:13:25.331
in a format nobody had decided on

442
00:13:25.331 --> 00:13:27.891
and with numbers nobody had provided.

443
00:13:27.891 --> 00:13:30.231
So she redid the request:

444
00:13:30.231 --> 00:13:31.971
read the README and the decisoes file

445
00:13:31.971 --> 00:13:33.191
in the config folder,

446
00:13:33.191 --> 00:13:35.091
